  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "The total number of six digit numbers, formed using the digits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;only and divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>, is _____ .",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "81",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given,</p><p>We have to form&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;digit number using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and for number should be divisible by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;which means that the addition of number should be divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;and unit place should be even, so&nbsp;we will make following cases:</p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;The number is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>444444</mn></math>&nbsp;which can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;way,</p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when the number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>,&nbsp;that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>3</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>, that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>, that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>5</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>, that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>6</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>, that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Case&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced></math>&nbsp;when number is formed using the digits&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></math>&nbsp;and last place is fixed with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>, that can be arranged in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math></p><p>Now adding all the cases we get,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>ways</mtext></math>.</p>",
